## Why This Is Asked

Examines ability to test dynamic, central policy-driven routing at scale and under failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Central policy propagation across distributed nodes
- Race conditions during config reloads
- Per-tenant overrides and canary validation
- Observability, metrics, and alerting for policy health

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify policy reloads propagate within short window
async function testPolicyPropagation(nodePool, policyApi) {
  await policyApi.forceUpdate();
  const results = await Promise.all(nodePool.map(n => n.getCurrentPolicyVersion()));
  return results.every(v => v === results[0]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate policy service latency and measure impact on SLAs?
- What tooling would you use to replay policy updates in canary regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design end-to-end API tests for dynamic feature flags and multi-region consistency under outages.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed config propagation latency
- Per-tenant isolation and cache invalidation
- Canary rollouts and rollback safety
- Observability and chaos testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify flag flip propagates across regions within TTL
async function testFlagPropagation(regions, tenantId, flagKey, expected) {
  const before = await fetchRecommendations(tenantId, regions[0]);
  await flipFlag(flagKey, true); // in etcd
  for (const r of regions) {
    const v = await pollUntilChanged(() => fetchRecommendations(tenantId, r), old => old !== before, 60000);
    if (v === before) throw new Error('Flag did not propagate in region ' + r);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure TTL impact on user-perceived latency during flag flips?
- How would you test partial outages where some regions see new flags while others don't?

Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical API-testing with event-driven, multi-tenant systems and guarantees like at-least-once delivery, idempotency, and DLQ routing.

Key Concepts
- Idempotency per tenant
- End-to-end delivery guarantees
- Failure modes: broker outage, consumer downtime
- Observability: audit logs and DLQs

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure duplicate webhook does not double-process
async function testIdempotentWebhook(httpPost, tenantId, event){
  const first = await httpPost(`/tenants/${tenantId}/webhooks`, event)
  const dup = await httpPost(`/tenants/${tenantId}/webhooks`, event)
  assert(first.status === 200 && dup.status === 200)
  // verify idempotent side-effects only occurred once
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate replay tests against a canary environment?
- How would you verify DLQ routing correctness under mixed failure modes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests must validate correctness and resilience in distributed, event-driven APIs. This question probes end-to-end reliability, idempotency, replay protection, and multi-region consistency under failures.

## Key Concepts

- Outbox pattern and event-bus propagation
- Idempotency keys and replay protection
- Out-of-order and duplicate event handling
- Partial regional failures and graceful degradation
- Eventually consistency SLAs and monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton using Node.js and supertest
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../src/app');

describe('Publish idempotency', () => {
  it('replays with same Idempotency-Key should not duplicate writes', async () => {
    const id = 'feed-42';
    const key = 'idem-42';
    await request(app).post(`/feeds/${id}/publish`).set('Idempotency-Key', key).send({ content: 'hello' });
    await request(app).post(`/feeds/${id}/publish`).set('Idempotency-Key', key).send({ content: 'hello' });
    // verify single write via read API or DB count
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model latency and jitter in the event bus during tests, and what are acceptable SLA thresholds?
- How would you extend tests to verify exactly-once delivery in the presence of network partitions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming API behavior under realistic conditions, including security, multi-tenant isolation, delivery guarantees, backpressure, and fault scenarios. It reveals depth in end-to-end testing of real-time systems.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket end-to-end testing
- JWT-based tenant isolation
- Ordering, deduplication, and reconnect semantics
- Backpressure and flow control
- Fault injection and resiliency testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example WebSocket test skeleton using ws library
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

// Test client for WebSocket validation
const testWebSocketConnection = async (tenantId, token) => {
  const ws = new WebSocket(`ws://localhost/ws/updates?token=${token}`);

  ws.on('open', () => {
    console.log(`Connected for tenant: ${tenantId}`);
  });

  ws.on('message', (data) => {
    // Validate tenant isolation and message ordering
    const message = JSON.parse(data);
    console.log(`Received: ${message.sequenceId} for tenant: ${tenantId}`);
  });

  return ws;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical API-testing skill with real-time feeds, multi-tenant isolation, and production-like failure modes. It also emphasizes contract testing between related services and resilience to downstream faults, all common in large tech ecosystems.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency testing under load with real-world patterns
- Per-tenant data isolation and data-ownership guarantees
- Consumer-driven contracts between interdependent APIs
- Resilience testing for downstream systems (brokers, queues) and retry/backoff strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// k6 basic latency test example
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const res = http.get('https://api.example.com/feed?tenant=tenantA&limit=50');
  check(res, { 'status 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you automate contract updates as API evolve, and what alerts would you set for contract violations?
- How would you detect potential data leakage across tenants during load testing and what safeguards would you implement?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical skills in testing feature-flag architectures at scale, including per-tenant isolation, dynamic rollouts, A/B experiments, and resilience across regions and caches.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and per-tenant gating
- Deterministic bucketing and fair distribution
- Cache invalidation and eventual consistency
- Cross-region propagation and latency
- Fault tolerance and graceful degradation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test skeleton (Jest + fetch-like API)
test('bucket stability under concurrent rollout updates', async () => {
  const results = await Promise.all([
    GET('/features/checkout', { tenant: 't1', user: 'u1' }),
    GET('/features/checkout', { tenant: 't1', user: 'u2' }),
    GET('/features/checkout', { tenant: 't2', user: 'u3' })
  ]);
  results.forEach(r => {
    expect(['on','off']).toContain(r.flag);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate partial outage of the central config store while a rollout is in progress and quantify impact on latency and correctness?
- What metrics would you collect to detect drift in bucketing over time and ensure fairness across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests reliability of composite APIs with dependent services.

## Key Concepts
- Downstream latency, timeouts, partial failures, circuit breakers
- Idempotent retries, exponential backoff, observability
- End-to-end timing and schema validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test using jest + nock
const nock = require('nock');
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../app');
test('partial data when one downstream times out', async () => {
  nock('http://sales').get('/daily').delay(3000).reply(200, {sales: 1000});
  nock('http://customers').get('/daily').reply(200, {customers: 50});
  const res = await request(app).get('/reports/daily');
  expect(res.statusCode).toBe(200);
  expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('sales');
  expect(res.headers).toHaveProperty('x-downstream-warn');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test circuit-breaker behavior under sustained latency?
- What metrics would you validate in your observability stack?

